{
  "abstract": "For a rational Diophantine septuple A, the regular-quintuple graph G(A) of paper 40 joins two entries when the other five form a regular quintuple. Paper 61 bounds G(A) by seven edges, conditionally, and paper 66 shows that a connected G(A) with a cycle is the hexagon with a pendant vertex or the heptagon. We close the two remaining seven-edge cases. An exhaustive enumeration of the 20 seven-edge triangle-free graphs on seven vertices, combined with the degree, K_{2,3} and connected-graph theorems of papers 55, 58, 59 and 66, leaves two disconnected graphs: H54 (atlas row 54, a theta graph θ(2,2,3) plus an isolated vertex) and H69 (row 69, θ(1,3,3) plus an isolated vertex). Paper 58 recorded both as having isolated F_p-points, not computed exactly. Both contain four-cycles, so the pivot equations of paper 60, valid globally by paper 61's matching corollary, cut them into finitely many characteristic-zero systems: three leaves for H54 and nine for H69. For H69 and two leaves of H54, msolve in characteristic 0 gives empty outputs or zero-dimensional sets whose rational-coefficient factors have degree 4 or 8. Every factor is root-free modulo a small prime, and every point satisfies the windows exactly in its field. The third H54 leaf was too large for a characteristic-0 run. Four modular computations show it has degree 40 and that its isolated-vertex coordinate is a root of (X²−4/9)(X²−1792/3249)(X²+36/25). Exact characteristic-0 slices along these factors account for all 40 points, none rational. So, conditional on msolve completeness and, for that one leaf, on four-prime agreement, every seven-edge G(A) is the hexagon with a pendant or the heptagon. No septuple is constructed.",
